# Machine Learning

Neural networks are inspired by the brain: layers of simple units that each
learn a small part of a much larger function. The interesting part is not any
single unit but **what falls out of stacking them**.

> A neural network is a differentiable program.

@image(
    src="cnn.png",
    alt="Diagram of a convolutional neural network",
    caption="A convolutional network, one block per stage",
    width=600
)

The diagram above shows the three stages: convolution, pooling, and a fully
connected head. Each stage is a function; training is `gradient descent` over
their composition.

## Layer sizes

@table(caption="Parameter count by layer")
Layer | Parameters | Notes
------|-----------:|------
Conv1 |        256 | 3x3 kernels
Conv2 |        512 | 3x3 kernels
FC    |     10,240 | the bulk of the model
@endtable

## Training loop

@code(language="python")
for batch in loader:
    loss = criterion(model(batch.x), batch.y)
    loss.backward()
    optimizer.step()
    optimizer.zero_grad()
@endcode

## The objective

@math
E = mc^2
@endmath

## Pipeline

@mermaid
graph TD
  A[Raw data] --> B[Features]
  B --> C[Model]
  C --> D[Predictions]
@endmermaid

